<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maria of Mangup</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">died 1477</span> · Also known as Maria Asanina Palaiologina</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maria of Mangup was a princess of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> principality of Theodoro in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> who married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-the-great">Stephen the Great</a> of Moldavia in 1472. The marriage linked Moldavia with the last Byzantine-ruled state in the Crimea, and Stephen supported her family's claims there. Her embroidered funeral cloth survives at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/putna-monastery">Putna Monastery</a>, where she was buried.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maria-of-mangup.jpg" alt="Maria of Mangup" width="320" height="566" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MariaPale%C3%B3loga.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
